{"id": "aa-docs/pg1228/10k/summary", "set": "aa-docs", "meta": {"book": "pg1228", "title": "On the Origin of Species By Means of Natural Selection", "context": 10000, "style": "summary", "document_tokens": 9948}, "thinking": true, "max_tokens": 512, "config": {"method": "plain", "K": 0, "weight_precision": "bf16", "kv_precision": "bf16", "drafter_precision": "bf16", "model": "google/gemma-4-31B-it", "run_label": "t0-fa3-diagnostic", "max_tokens_override": 512, "metrics_settle_seconds": 6, "requests_sha256": "7abb2f93587b01649c27e262e041afe96dd68cc055908d80f35610b5de9506ae", "return_token_ids": true, "environment_sha256": "6d011ff07ab7c6ec1f331aac36db968b38c4ec39c2147eb86ba069da9f861ac7", "seed": 1}, "turns": [{"content": "", "reasoning": "A substantial excerpt from Charles Darwin's *On the Origin of Species* (Introduction and Chapter 1).\nDetailed reportalal of at least 2,000 words.\n\n        *   Summarize content in order.\n        *   Explain main themes/arguments.\n\n    *   *Introduction:*\n        *   Darwin's background: HMS Beagle voyage, interest in South American distribution.\n        *   Timeline: 1837 (initial thoughts), 1844 (sketch), current publication (abstract).\n        *   Influence of Alfred Russel Wallace (parallel discovery).\n        *   Purpose of the book: To explain the \"mystery of mysteries\" (origin of species).\n        *   Methodology: Using evidence from domestication, nature, and the geological record.\n        *   Core Thesis: Species are not immutable; they descend from common ancestors via Natural Selection.\n\n    *   *Chapter 1: Variation Under Domestication:*\n        *   Observation: Domesticated plants/animals vary more than wild ones.\n        *   Causes of variability: Environmental changes, nutrition, reproductive system sensitivity.\n        *   The role of \"sports\" (sudden mutations).\n        *   Influence of habit/use (e.g., domestic duck bones).\n        *   Correlation of growth (one change leads to others).\n        *   Inheritance: The principle that \"like produces like,\" but with variations.\n        *   Reversion: Discussion on whether domestic own revert to wild types (Darwin argues they don't necessarily).\n        *   Comparison between domestic varieties and species: They are fundamentally similar in terms of variational patterns.\n        *   Origin of breeds: Debate over whether breeds come from one or many wild species (e.g., dogs).\n\n    *   *Introduction:* Set the stage. Identify the text as a foundational piece of biological science.\n    *   *Section 1: Sequential Summary (The \"In Order\" part):*\n        *   Break down the Introduction.\n        *   Break down the Table of Contents (as it outlines the rest of the book).\n        *   Break down Chapter 1.\n    *   *Section 2: Analysis of Main Themes/Arguments:*\n        *   The Concept of Variation.\n        *   The Role of Selection (Artificial vs. Natural).\n        *   The Rejection of Immutability.", "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 9998, "total_tokens": 10510, "completion_tokens": 512, "completion_tokens_details": {"reasoning_tokens": 511}}, "finish_reason": "length", "provider": "local-vllm", "model": "google/gemma-4-31B-it", "elapsed_s": 23.670665867626667, "prompt_tokens": 9998, "completion_tokens": 512, "chunk_arrivals_s": [5.278684671968222, 5.278785463422537, 5.305063836276531, 5.340156264603138, 5.375740533694625, 5.411924151703715, 5.447221662849188, 5.482783067971468, 5.518204417079687, 5.553915059193969, 5.589727280661464, 5.62536308541894, 5.660964271053672, 5.69673927128315, 5.7327821515500546, 5.768147647380829, 5.8038277346640825, 5.839589100331068, 5.875113243237138, 5.910786798223853, 5.946370899677277, 5.981865990906954, 6.017734436318278, 6.053216308355331, 6.089289098978043, 6.124792451038957, 6.160513011738658, 6.1962357722222805, 6.231968276202679, 6.267383167520165, 6.302871439605951, 6.338563719764352, 6.374290691688657, 6.409566335380077, 6.445715179666877, 6.4814667869359255, 6.5168